NAME
    man - display reference manual pages

SYNOPSIS
    man [NAME...]
    man [-l | --list]
    man [-h | --help]

DESCRIPTION
    Displays the manual page for each NAME on standard output.
    With no arguments (or with -l) it lists every documented
    command on the system with a one-line summary, which doubles
    as a quick way to discover what is installed.

    When the output is a terminal the page is shown in a pager
    instead: a white status bar on the last row reads
        Manual page vcc(1) line 12 (press h for help or q to quit)
    and names the page and the first line you are looking at. When
    the output is redirected the page is simply printed instead, so
    'man ls > /tmp/ls.txt' leaves you a file to grep.

KEYS
    Up / Down      scroll one line (k and j do the same)
    space, f, Enter  scroll down one screen (also Ctrl+F, Ctrl+D)
    b, u           scroll up one screen (also Ctrl+B)
    Home, g        jump to the first line of the page
    End, G         jump to the last line of the page
    h              list the keys (any other key goes back)
    q              quit and return to the shell (Esc, Ctrl+C too)

EXAMPLES
    man ls          page for the ls command
    man useradd id  two pages at once
    man             full listing of documented commands
    man vcc > /tmp/v.txt  the page as a plain file, no pager

SEE ALSO
    Every command in the system has a page; try man vash,
    man vedit, man coreutils. AGENTS.md at the repo root rules
    that new commands must come with a page.
